<BaseLayout title="Editorial policy" description="Our approach to technology research, testing, product comparisons, affiliate independence and corrections.">
  <div class="content-container simple-page prose"><span class="eyebrow">How our work is done</span><h1>Our editorial policy</h1><p class="intro">Helpful recommendations should start with the reader's requirements, not a referral commission.</p>
    <h2>Research and testing</h2><p>We compare official product documentation, published specifications, compatibility requirements and practical usage scenarios. We label desk-researched articles accurately. We only claim hands-on testing when we have performed it and can document the method.</p>
    <h2>How recommendations are formed</h2><p>We assess suitability for different audiences and budgets, explain compromises and mention reasons someone should avoid a product. We do not create fictitious customer reviews, first-hand observations or performance benchmarks.</p>
    <h2>Prices and availability</h2><p>Amazon prices and stock can change rapidly. We do not scrape Amazon content or present unverified live prices. Current prices, when supported by approved programme tools in a future release, will follow applicable Amazon rules.</p>
    <h2>Affiliate relationships</h2><p>Some future links may earn commissions. Editorial content and disclosures remain visible regardless of monetization. We will identify paid affiliate links near the link and disclose our Associate participation.</p>
    <h2>Corrections and updates</h2><p>Article update dates reflect substantive review. If a technical claim becomes outdated, we aim to correct it and explain meaningful changes. See <a href="/contact/">Contact</a> for future correction-submission details.</p></div>
</BaseLayout>
